# Trivy ignore file for the production image scan (.github/workflows/docker-scan.yml).
#
# Same philosophy as the `pnpm audit` allowlist in package.json / SECURITY.md:
# mute ONLY findings that are not reachable in the shipped runtime, each with a
# rationale and a review date. The image scan runs `--ignore-unfixed`, so
# unfixable base-OS CVEs are already excluded; this file is for FIXABLE findings
# that live in base-image tooling the runtime never invokes.
#
# The runner stage executes `node server.js` (the Next.js standalone server) as
# an unprivileged user. The bundled `npm` CLI (and its own dependencies) ship in
# the node base image but are never run in production.

# picomatch ReDoS via crafted extglob patterns — bundled inside the base image's
# npm CLI (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/...). npm is not invoked at runtime,
# and the app's own dependency tree is gated separately by `pnpm audit`.
# Review by 2026-09-18 (drop when the node base image bumps its bundled npm).
CVE-2026-33671

# sigstore certificateOIDs verification-constraint bypass (CVE-2026-48815, HIGH;
# fixed in sigstore 4.1.1). Same class as CVE-2026-33671 above — bundled inside
# the base image's npm CLI (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/sigstore/,
# used by npm for package provenance). NOT in this app's dependency tree (absent
# from pnpm-lock.yaml), and npm is never invoked in the production runtime
# (`node server.js` only). The app's own deps are gated separately by `pnpm audit`.
# Review by 2026-10-03 (drop when the node base image bumps its bundled npm).
CVE-2026-48815
